WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:05.250
The simple,

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.950
experimental approach that Luria took, which is to take a population 

00:00:09.540 --> 00:00:10.450
of--

00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:15.430
to take a bacteria cell and to grow a 

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.420
population of bacteria that are descended from that cell in liquid culture, so that 

00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:23.570
you go from one cell to billions, all in a test tube full of liquid,

00:00:23.930 --> 00:00:28.720
and then to spread those cells out onto a Petri dish that 

00:00:28.730 --> 00:00:30.510
contained the virus T1.

00:00:31.740 --> 00:00:32.180
Normally,

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:37.020
if you put a lot of bacteria on the surface of a Petri dish which contains food for 

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:37.330
them,

00:00:37.630 --> 00:00:39.500
they will proliferate on the plate.

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:44.500
So you'll see lots of bacteria growing all over the surface of your plate.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:50.050
And what happens though, if you coat that plate with this virus T1,

00:00:50.940 --> 00:00:52.670
almost all the bacteria are killed,

00:00:52.670 --> 00:00:53.310
as I said.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.880
So you actually see, in contrast to when you have no T1,

00:00:56.120 --> 00:00:58.410
there's actually almost no growth of bacteria on the plate.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:02.450
But in a few places where a cell is resistant to this virus,

00:01:03.290 --> 00:01:05.620
what will happen is that cell will now proliferate,

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:08.050
generating millions and billions of progeny,

00:01:08.440 --> 00:01:13.170
which will form a mound of bacterial cells that you can actually see what's called a 

00:01:13.170 --> 00:01:15.880
colony, a round mound of cells,

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:20.510
which all derive from the original cell, that one that didn't get killed by the virus.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:24.130
So in the induced model,

00:01:24.190 --> 00:01:28.350
the prediction was that all the cultures would have,

00:01:28.840 --> 00:01:29.600
let's say,

00:01:29.770 --> 00:01:32.860
anywhere from one to five resistant cells.

00:01:34.810 --> 00:01:38.890
While in the spontaneous model, he realized that 

00:01:41.830 --> 00:01:42.640
some of the cells,

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:45.070
some of the cultures , might have zero, some might have one,

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:49.830
and some might have fifty. So why did Luria think that these distributions would 

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:50.780
be so different?

00:01:52.020 --> 00:01:56.760
And the reason he thought they would be very different 

00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:01.310
is because in the induced model, the simplest one to start with, all the 

00:02:01.310 --> 00:02:05.920
bacteria that one is going to spread on the surface of the plate, prior to ever seeing T1, 

00:02:06.220 --> 00:02:10.950
are going to be sensitive to the virus because they've never seen the virus so they can't 

00:02:10.960 --> 00:02:14.600
adapt their genetic material in the absence of ever seeing T1.

00:02:15.570 --> 00:02:19.480
So what one expects to see is on the surface of the plate 

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:24.860
rare cells that have spotted the virus that have acquired the 

00:02:24.860 --> 00:02:25.230
trait.

00:02:25.710 --> 00:02:30.600
So if one did a series of plates, one would expect that those rare events would happen sort 

00:02:30.600 --> 00:02:32.370
of at similar rates on the different plates,

00:02:32.370 --> 00:02:36.880
so each plate would have the same number of mounds of cells, or colonies.

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:38.780
So if it was,

00:02:39.190 --> 00:02:43.970
let's just give an example that it's one in a million 

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:48.380
chance, and you put ten plates each which have 

00:02:49.270 --> 00:02:50.970
ten million bacterium on them,

00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:52.280
then you would expect,

00:02:52.770 --> 00:02:53.480
on average,

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:59.100
ten of the bacteria exposed to become resistant to the 

00:02:59.230 --> 00:02:59.830
virus.

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:00.750
Now,

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:05.700
if you-- in the spontaneous model where limitations are 

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:08.880
happening all the time,

00:03:10.840 --> 00:03:15.750
one could imagine that these bacteria are growing up, they're having mistakes in their 

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:19.950
DNA or in the chemistry that's leading to mistakes to their genetic material,

00:03:20.140 --> 00:03:24.080
some of those changes are going to cause the bacteria to maybe grow a little faster,

00:03:24.080 --> 00:03:25.880
some are going to make them be a little bit different shape,

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:29.480
but there's occasional genetic change that is going to make them resistant to the virus.

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:34.080
And that can happen at any time during the growth of this culture.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:40.290
So let's imagine that it happens when there are only 100,000 cells in the culture.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:44.830
The culture has to go through a series of-- the cells in the culture have to go through more 

00:03:44.830 --> 00:03:48.310
divisions in order to generate millions of cells,

00:03:48.750 --> 00:03:53.280
and that particular bacterium that's become resistant has the chance to make more of 

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:53.780
itself.

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.780
It'll make ten bacteria that are now resistant.

00:03:57.600 --> 00:04:02.238
And so the fact that the spontaneous change can happen 

00:04:02.898 --> 00:04:07.748
at any time during the growth of the culture, it's not confined to when it actually sees the virus,

00:04:08.098 --> 00:04:09.828
you can see how you could get, in fact,

00:04:10.128 --> 00:04:14.688
many more resistant cells in a culture than you might expect otherwise.

00:04:15.778 --> 00:04:20.088
So Luria's results of the distributions of 

00:04:20.328 --> 00:04:25.048
resistant cells in the cultures was very skewed and not consistent amongst all the 

00:04:25.048 --> 00:04:25.618
cultures,

00:04:28.018 --> 00:04:31.478
ruled out the induced model.

00:04:32.948 --> 00:04:36.908
It was consistent with the results for the spontaneous model.

00:04:37.978 --> 00:04:41.068
But in and of itself didn't predict the distributions,

00:04:41.138 --> 00:04:42.688
as Luria originally figured out.

00:04:42.688 --> 00:04:44.188
He just said that they should be very,

00:04:44.678 --> 00:04:45.788
very skewed.

00:04:46.538 --> 00:04:50.798
And so he called in his friend Max Delbrück to help him with the math,

00:04:50.888 --> 00:04:51.548
to say,

00:04:52.178 --> 00:04:55.388
can we develop a mathematical formula that will actually,

00:04:55.978 --> 00:05:00.468
assuming they're spontaneous mutations, that will actually give rise to 

00:05:01.088 --> 00:05:03.088
the skewed distributions that we actually see?

00:05:03.378 --> 00:05:04.648
So it was taking it from being just consistent, to in fact, 

00:05:04.649 --> 00:05:10.648
no, we can actually predict that the results are actually consistent, 

00:05:10.649 --> 00:05:15.648
that they actually show that this is happening by spontaneous mutations. 

